+What the receiver needs: request_delivery takes domain_id, commit, interface, environment, approver and branch, all strings, and creates a DeliveryRequest (stage requested) that shows, in replaced_commit, the commit the environment pointed at (not sent by Authoring; Delivery reads it from its own Environment). A request with no branch is refused (precondition `branch-stated`).

A.1.0. {"problem": "replaced_commit is read from Environment.current_commit, but no declared Delivery action ever updates that property. record_deployment sets only deployed and refusal, and Delivery has no action that writes Environment.current_commit. Unless the kernel changes it outside the declaration, it will not move after a deploy, so later requests would show a stale commit as the one they replaced. The change also does not say who keeps current_commit up to date.", "where": "SKILL.md, Delivery objects (Environment) and request_delivery effect; record_deployment", "kind": "behaviour"}
A.1.1. {"problem": "replaced_commit is captured once, when the request is received, but the field is described as 'what this delivery replaces' and is meant to show what was running before the deploy. Several requests can be open for one environment, and a request can be re-previewed and re-deployed from done. If another delivery lands in between, or the request is for the commit already running, the stored value no longer shows what was running at deploy time. The stated purpose, seeing what was running before at a glance, is then not met.", "where": "SKILL.md, DeliveryRequest.replaced_commit and request_delivery effect", "kind": "behaviour"}
A.2.0. {"problem": "The change reads Environment.current_commit, but no Delivery action ever writes it. record_deployment sets only deployed and refusal, so current_commit is never updated after a deploy. replaced_commit would therefore stay unset, or show a stale commit, in real operation. It would be correct only where the Environment fixture was seeded by hand, as in the new example. This defeats the stated goal of seeing what was running before.", "where": "SKILL.md, request_delivery Effect and the Environment object; record_deployment (no effect on current_commit)", "kind": "behaviour"}
A.2.1. {"problem": "replaced_commit is captured once, when the request is received, but the deployer wants to know what was running before the deploy. If several requests for one environment are open, or an earlier one deploys in between, the value goes stale and names a commit that this deploy does not actually replace. The field is also set on a request that is never deployed or whose deploy is refused, so its name and description overstate what it shows.", "where": "SKILL.md, DeliveryRequest.replaced_commit and request_delivery Effect", "kind": "behaviour"}
A.2.2. {"problem": "The case where no Environment named params.environment exists is not specified. Environment has no link to DeliveryRequest and is matched only by name string. The text says 'if it has one' only for current_commit, so a missing Environment is undefined. The new example covers only the case where it is present, with no example for the unset case.", "where": "SKILL.md, request_delivery Effect; EXAMPLES.md, a-delivery-request-shows-the-commit-it-replaced", "kind": "other"}
A.3.0. {"problem": "The new replaced_commit field is read from Environment.current_commit, but nothing in the skill ever sets that property. record_deployment only sets deployed and refusal on the DeliveryRequest, and no action creates or updates an Environment. Real deployments therefore never move current_commit, so replaced_commit would stay unset or go stale, and deployers would not see what was running before. The new example only works because its fixture hand-sets current_commit.", "where": "SKILL.md Delivery actions: request_delivery Effect, record_deployment, and the Environment object; EXAMPLES.md a-delivery-request-shows-the-commit-it-replaced", "kind": "behaviour"}
A.3.1. {"problem": "replaced_commit is fixed when the request is received. The stated purpose is to show what was running before the deploy. If another delivery to the same environment is deployed between the request and its own deploy, the value is wrong. A request can also be previewed or redeployed (done to deploying), and replaced_commit is never refreshed at deploy time.", "where": "SKILL.md DeliveryRequest.replaced_commit and the request_delivery Effect", "kind": "behaviour"}
A.3.2. {"problem": "The header gives the Delivery domain as version 1 with interface 1. The change adds a property and a new effect to the Delivery declaration without bumping the domain version, while Authoring's version is tracked more carefully. The version line no longer matches the changed declaration.", "where": "SKILL.md header line (delivery domain version 1)", "kind": "reference"}
A.4.0. {"problem": "replaced_commit is captured once, when the request is received, but the request can be deployed much later. If another delivery to the same environment is deployed in between (or two requests are queued), the stored value no longer names what the deploy actually replaces. Also, if the environment already points at the request's own commit, replaced_commit equals the commit being delivered. This does not meet the stated aim of showing the deployer what was running before.", "where": "SKILL.md, request_delivery Effect and the DeliveryRequest.replaced_commit description ('when the request was received')", "kind": "behaviour"}
A.4.1. {"problem": "The feature relies on Environment.current_commit being accurate, but no Delivery action or transition ever sets it. record_deployment only sets deployed and refusal, and the stage transitions do not touch the Environment. After a successful deploy the environment still points at the old commit, so later requests would show a stale replaced_commit. The Environment object also has no domain_id, so it is unclear whose commit it holds when several domains deliver to the same environment name.", "where": "SKILL.md, Environment object, record_deployment, request_delivery Effect", "kind": "behaviour"}
A.4.2. {"problem": "A property was added to DeliveryRequest and the handoff description was changed, but the delivery domain version is still stated as 1 (interface 1) in the header. The change also adds a lookup of Environment inside request_delivery. The new example only covers an environment with a current commit, not the case where the environment has none or does not exist, which the rule says leaves replaced_commit unset.", "where": "SKILL.md, header (delivery domain version 1); EXAMPLES.md a-delivery-request-shows-the-commit-it-replaced", "kind": "other"}
A.5.0. {"problem": "replaced_commit is copied from Environment.current_commit, but no Delivery action ever sets current_commit. record_deployment only sets deployed and refusal, and nothing creates or updates an Environment. In a real deployment the field would always be unset or stale, so the request would not show what was running before. The example only works because it starts from a hand-made fixture with current_commit already set.", "where": "SKILL.md, Delivery actions: request_delivery Effect, record_deployment and the Environment object; EXAMPLES.md, a-delivery-request-shows-the-commit-it-replaced", "kind": "behaviour"}
A.5.1. {"problem": "The value is captured when the request is received, not when the deploy happens. If another delivery to the same environment deploys in between, or two requests are open at once, replaced_commit shows a commit that is not the one actually replaced. The stated purpose is that the deployer sees what was running before, so this undermines it.", "where": "SKILL.md, DeliveryRequest.replaced_commit and request_delivery Effect", "kind": "behaviour"}
A.5.2. {"problem": "The case where the named Environment does not exist is not defined. Authoring only offers local and staging, but request_delivery accepts any string for environment, and Delivery declares no way to create Environments. The Effect says 'if it has one', which covers a missing current_commit but not a missing Environment. The Effect also looks up another object by name, which no other action in the skill does.", "where": "SKILL.md, request_delivery Effect; Environment object", "kind": "other"}
A.5.3. {"problem": "The new example covers only the case where the environment has a current commit. It does not cover an environment with no current_commit, where replaced_commit should stay unset, even though the rule describes that case. The existing a-delivery-request-must-say-its-branch example, with no Environment in its start state, now has undefined replaced_commit behaviour.", "where": "EXAMPLES.md, Delivery scenarios", "kind": "reference"}

